1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: Swift-footed Achilles, the greatest of the Greek heroes, is racing a tortoise. And the tortoise has a head start.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERB: That hardly seems fair. On the tortoise, I mean.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: Ah, but listen. Each time Achilles reaches the spot where the tortoise was, it has crept a little further on.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: So, says an argument nearly 2,500 years old, he can never catch it.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: Which is plainly wrong. Of course he catches it.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: Sure. But saying exactly what's wrong with the argument took more than 2,000 years, and some philosophers say it still isn't settled.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: This is Zeno's paradox of Achilles and the tortoise.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERB: Zeno was born around 490 BC in Ellia, a Greek city on the west coast of southern Italy.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: And he was a student and friend of an older philosopher, Parmenides.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERB: Parmenides taught that reality is one unchanging whole, and that change and motion are illusions.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERA: Illusions? Motion itself?

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERB: Motion itself. You can imagine how that went down.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: Critics found it absurd. So Zeno turned the tables, showing that their views, followed through, led to worse absurdities.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERB: That style of argument is called reductio ad absurdum, and Aristotle called Zeno the inventor of dialectic.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: Sadly, Zeno's book is lost. Fewer than two hundred of his own words survive.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: So we know his arguments from others, such as Plato, Aristotle, and Simplishius, writing a thousand years later.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: Plato even has the two visit Athens to meet a young Socrates, though he probably invented that meeting.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERB: A good scene, then, but probably not history.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERA: The most famous argument is a race. Aristotle gives it in his Physics, like this.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERB: In a race, the quickest runner can never overtake the slowest, since the pursuer must first reach the point whence the pursued started,
